00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.030 align:start position:0%
 
hi there  so this is the day before i leave japan  it's been a phenomenal visit here  the conference in fukuoka was great lots  of fabulous polyglots japanese and  from other asian countries i'm sure glad  we did the polyglot conference in asia i  think they're probably going to organize  their own polyglot conferences  it's so heartening to see so many young  people learning three four five  languages it doesn't matter if you're  japanese or chinese or  any kind of ease american ease  canadianese everybody can learn and  they're living proof of that so that's  quite exciting  i had some chats with  language related uh  video youtubers  and i hopefully will do more in the  future  and behind me  is the you can just make it out is the  marazzan bookstore  seven floors  i was in there i like to visit  bookstores i went to the language  department it's astounding how much  space  is devoted to tests it seems that  conventional language learning here has  more to do with how to pass a test than  learning the language  i'll show you some pictures snapshots  that i took inside  i think this is gonna has to change  it'll change slowly it's like learning  languages it happens slowly  but there are people who see things  differently  uh i was at a uh  call it a cram school  where they're using extensive reading as  their  main way of teaching kids english and  the results are much better than the  traditional way and if people read a lot  then they don't need to do these toeic  books and  the top 1000 2 000 3000 words the key  grammar  issues in toeic you don't need that if  you've absorbed the language through  lots of reading  and  for those of you who follow me on  instagram or  you or at least facebook you'll see the  snapshot i took of that school  i visited with there's a group of  polyglots in tokyo who call themselves  the trilinguals because you're only  allowed to join the group if you speak  more than two languages  and they have various things they do  like speaking in three or four languages  with each other and translating for each  other into different languages not their  native language so that was a lot of fun  and  generally i think that  japan is a very entrepreneurial society  we tend to think of japan as the land of  the mitsubishis and the nissans but it's  very entrepreneurial and i'm starting to  see i mean it's probably been a long  time developing but i  felt it this time young entrepreneurs in  language in the internet people who  understand that that we don't need  all of these books with essentially the  same content i looked at some of the  japanese books for learning other  languages there's more japanese in those  books than there is the target language  uh the so yeah i think things are going  to change we want to be part of that  change so with that in mind uh tomorrow  we fly back mark and i  uh to vancouver  [Music]  what  [Music]  so  [Music]  you

